omplete years: Erie's average low shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.00 to +0.13) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Erie's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.23), about +2.6\u00b0F since 1874. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.56\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.97) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.57\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.97). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 4 of 12 months. Highs rose in 9 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Erie has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Erie's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.51 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.27 to +0.75), about +7.7 inches since 1874. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 42.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 1977 (61.70 in); the driest, 1934 (23.84 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}